<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chutia kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1187 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Sadiya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the upper Brahmaputra valley centred on Sadiya, the main rival of the Ahom kingdom until its conquest in 1524.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chutia kingdom took shape in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a>. Its traditional founding is dated to 1187 under a ruler remembered as Birpal, though the early history rests mainly on later chronicles. By the 14th century it was a significant power north of the river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chutia-people">Chutias</a> were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodo-kachari-peoples">Bodo-Kachari</a> people whose rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> forms of kingship, patronising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> worship, notably the Tamreswari temple near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a>-plate inscriptions of the 14th and 15th centuries record land grants and use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and early Assamese. The kingdom was known for metalwork, including the manufacture of guns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>, and for its control of trade routes to the hills.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a long series of wars, the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chutia-people">Chutias</a> and annexed their kingdom in 1523 to 1524. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> became the seat of an Ahom frontier governor, the Sadiya-khowa Gohain, and many Chutia nobles and artisans were absorbed into Ahom society.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1187 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.32%;width:6.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1187 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sadiya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dhirnarayan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaktism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Assamese, Deori</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chutiya kingdom, Sadiya kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
